":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"7","title":"No Taxpayer Funding for Abortion and Abortion Insurance Full Disclosure Act of 2025","introducedDate":"2025-01-22","latestActionDate":"2025-01-22","latestActionText":"Referred to the Committee on Energy and Commerce, and in addition to the Committees on the Judiciary, and Ways and Means, for a period to be subsequently determined by the Speaker, in each case for consideration of such provisions as fall within the jurisdiction of the committee concerned.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Abortion","Appropriations","Assault and harassment offenses","Comprehensive health care","Consumer affairs","Crimes against women","Employee benefits and pensions","Government buildings, facilities, and property","Health care costs and insurance","Health care coverage and access","Health facilities and institutions","Health programs administration and funding"],"sponsorBioguideId":"S000522","cosponsorCount":109,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/7","industries":[{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly restricts what health insurance plans may cover and how federal subsidies interact with abortion coverage in qualified health plans."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill prohibits federal health care facilities and federal employees from providing abortion services, affecting facilities that currently receive federal funds."},{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Organizations focused on reproductive health policy and abortion access or restrictions have a direct stake in the regulatory framework this bill establishes."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would make permanent the restrictions on federal funding for abortion services and health insurance coverage that includes abortion. Currently, these restrictions are renewed each year through appropriations bills; the bill would apply them across all federal programs and funds. It would also prohibit qualified health plans offered through insurance marketplaces from covering abortion. The bill includes exceptions for cases of rape, incest, or when the pregnant person's life is in danger.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys health insurance. Anyone who pays a medical bill.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"B001260","score":0.061260165720508956},"overlapCount":107},"meaning":{"percentile":46.2170273258569,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.8157541333075669}},{"billId":"119-hr-29","bioguideId":"H001091","cycle":2026,"score":0.007364730855310711,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":69727.47,"donorShare":0.015162681172698523,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.007364730855310711}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.8157541333075669},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-29","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"29","title":"Laken Riley Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-02-10","latestActionText":"Read the second time. Placed on Senate Legislative Calendar under General Orders. Calendar No. 10.","policyArea":"Immigration","subjects":["Border security and unlawful immigration","Civil actions and liability","Crimes against property","Criminal investigation, prosecution, interrogation","Detention of persons","Immigration status and procedures","Intergovernmental relations","Refugees, asylum, displaced persons","State and local government operations","Visas and passports"],"sponsorBioguideId":"C001129","cosponsorCount":53,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/29","industries":[{"industry":"government","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill directly imposes new detention mandates on DHS and creates new litigation exposure for federal agencies, affecting federal and state government operations and budgets."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill creates new grounds for state litigation against the federal government and expands detention requirements, increasing demand for legal services in immigration and intergovernmental litigation."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would require the Department of Homeland Security to detain individuals who are in the country without authorization and have been arrested for or charged with burglary, theft, larceny, or shoplifting. It would also allow state governments to sue the federal government for injunctive relief when federal immigration enforcement decisions or failures—such as releasing individuals from custody or not fulfilling visa or asylum processing requirements—cause harm to the state or its residents, including financial harm exceeding $100.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People applying to live here, and their families here. People applying to come here, and their families here.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"S000250","score":0.02595346470272257},"overlapCount":50},"meaning":{"percentile":35.82372453009003,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.8157541333075669}},{"billId":"119-hr-6143","bioguideId":"H001091","cycle":2026,"score":0.0065306306185715784,"matches":[{"industry":"agriculture","donorAmount":57500,"donorShare":0.01250374016768664,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.005054703472043535},{"industry":"tech","donorAmount":16500,"donorShare":0.0035880297872492094,"billConfidence":0.75,"contribution":0.00114511588954762},{"industry":"manufacturing","donorAmount":5500,"donorShare":0.0011960099290830699,"billConfidence":0.65,"contribution":0.00033081125698042357}